t rivals major corporations and even some national currencies. That's not a guarantee of future success, but it's a sign that it's not just a fringe idea. It's already a real-world system that people rely on for savings, payments, and even remittances across borders. 4. **I'd also say: skepticism is healthy.** I'm not asking you to blindly accept Bitcoin. I'm asking you to consider that the current global monetary system—with its inflation, its bailouts, its capital controls, and its exclusion of billions of people—also has no "real-world backing" in the sense of being physically valuable. It works because we all agree it works. Bitcoin is simply a different agreement, one that is transparent, global, and voluntary. You don't have to buy that argument today. But I believe that as people continue to experience the downsides of fiat—whether it's inflation, frozen bank accounts, or currency devaluation—more and more will find Bitcoin's alternative worth exploring. That's not a clever justification. It's a prediction based on the incentives that the system creates.
