23:01:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Of course. Let me try to make it as simple as I can. Imagine you own a plot of land in a growing city. One week, a rumor spreads that the city might rezone the area, and the price of your land drops 30%. The next week, the rumor turns out to be false, and the price bounces back. The land itself didn't change—it's still the same size, in the same location, with the same potential. What changed was people's emotions and short-term expectations. Bitcoin is similar. Its **price** goes up and down wildly because it's still new, relatively small, and driven by speculation and fear. But its **fundamental properties**—the things that give it long-term value—don't change with the price. Those properties are: - **Scarce:** Only 21 million will ever exist. No one can print more. - **Portable:** You can send millions of dollars across the world in minutes. - **Verifiable:** Anyone can check the ledger to confirm ownership. - **Decentralized:** No government, bank, or company controls it. These properties are valuable, and they don't disappear when the price drops. Over the long term—meaning years, not weeks—more people recognize that value, adoption grows, and the price tends to rise. The 30% drops are scary, but they've happened many times before, and each time, the price eventually recovered and went higher. Think of it like a rollercoaster that climbs a mountain. The ride is bumpy and scary, but over time, you end up higher than where you started. For someone saving for retirement 20 years from now, the bumps along the way don't matter as long as the long-term trend is up. So in simple terms: Bitcoin's **price is volatile**, but its **value as a scarce, global, and unstoppable asset is stable**. That's why people believe it can be a reliable store of value over the long term—not because it never goes down, but because its fundamental properties are durable and increasingly sought after.
