hat your friends' losses don't matter, or that Bitcoin is perfect, or that everyone should buy it. What I will say is this: Bitcoin's long-term viability rests on its properties—scarcity, decentralization, verifiability—not on whether every person who touches it uses it wisely. The system itself is sound. But it is not forgiving. And until it becomes less volatile and more user-friendly, it will continue to hurt people who don't treat it with care. If that means my argument feels weaker to you, I understand. But I'd rather be honest about the risks than pretend Bitcoin is a magic solution that works for everyone right now.
